**Reused Evidence**
None.

**New Evidence**
Exposure profile for 2022: `material_exposure` with real choices on doctrine, signaling, diplomacy, export controls, and crisis response; technical custody and arsenal opacity were partly institutional, not fully PM-controlled.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PM meets President of Russian Federation in Samarkand, Uzbekistan","publisher":"Prime Minister of India","publication_date":"2022-09-16","url":"https://www.pmindia.gov.in/en/news_updates/pm-meets-president-of-russian-federation-in-samarkand-uzbekistan/","claim":"In September 2022, Modi told Putin that the Ukraine war should end soon and that dialogue and diplomacy were necessary.","locator":"lines 75-80","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PMO record of a dated statement by Modi while in office.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 ruler period.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler statement in his capacity as Prime Minister.","contrary_evidence":["The same meeting also covered bilateral cooperation on trade, energy, and defence, so it is evidence of foreign-policy positioning rather than nuclear-risk management alone."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.8","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement on accidental firing of missile","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-03-11","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1805148&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"The Defence Ministry said a routine-maintenance technical malfunction caused an accidental missile firing into Pakistan and ordered a high-level Court of Inquiry.","locator":"lines 13-16","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government statement released the day after the incident.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and squarely on the ruler-period safety question.","ruler_attribution":"Government action under Modi's executive authority; the statement reflects institutional response, not a personal order.","contrary_evidence":["The statement does not identify a specific launcher or command-chain breakdown, so it is not proof of personal negligence or intent."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.8","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India says it accidentally fired missile into Pakistan","publisher":"Reuters via RFE/RL","publication_date":"2022-03-11","url":"https://www.rferl.org/a/pakistan-india-missile-mystery-object-airspace/31747903.html","claim":"Reuters reporting said the accidental launch raised questions about safety mechanisms and could have endangered passenger flights and civilians.","locator":"lead paragraphs of the Reuters report","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters-sourced contemporaneous reporting adds independent corroboration to the official admission.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and tied to the same incident as the official statement.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects the consequences of the Indian state apparatus under Modi's tenure, not a direct ruler order.","contrary_evidence":["The article also notes that the missile was unarmed and that the incident was framed as accidental, so it does not by itself show deliberate escalation."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.8","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Striking Asymmetries: Nuclear Transitions in Southern Asia","publisher":"Carnegie Endowment for International Peace","publication_date":"2022-07","url":"https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2022/07/striking-asymmetries-nuclear-transitions-in-southern-asia?lang=en","claim":"The article says India’s nuclear release authority remains with civilian authorities and that only the prime minister and designated successors can order nuclear use.","locator":"line 1601","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Analytical scholarship, not primary law, but it directly summarizes India’s command-and-control arrangement and is dated to the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2022 and directly relevant to the ruler-period authority structure.","ruler_attribution":"Civilian command is a real authority attribute of the PM; the passage speaks to what Modi could personally authorize.","contrary_evidence":["This is doctrine/structure, not evidence that Modi exercised the authority in 2022 in a risk-reducing way."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.8","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"10. World nuclear forces","publisher":"Stockholm International Peace Research Institute","publication_date":"2022-06-13","url":"https://www.sipri.org/yearbook/2022/10","claim":"SIPRI said India and Pakistan appeared to be increasing the size of their nuclear inventories and that India provided little public information about arsenal status or size.","locator":"lines 93 and 100-101","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Highly cited open-source nuclear inventory assessment from a top arms-control institute.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2022 and describes the inherited nuclear baseline facing Modi during the year.","ruler_attribution":"This is inherited posture and state-level capability, not a personal policy choice by Modi alone.","contrary_evidence":["SIPRI is an estimate-based open-source assessment, so the exact warhead count is uncertain."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nuclear Notebook: How many nuclear weapons does India have in 2022?","publisher":"Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists","publication_date":"2022-07-11","url":"https://thebulletin.org/premium/2022-07/nuclear-notebook-how-many-nuclear-weapons-does-india-have-in-2022/","claim":"The notebook says India continued modernizing its arsenal, had several systems under development, and maintained a relatively opaque nuclear-information environment.","locator":"lines 128-138 and 132-136","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist open-source nuclear inventory analysis with explicit 2022 force assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year and useful for inherited baseline and end-of-year posture.","ruler_attribution":"This is a state-posture assessment under Modi’s tenure, not proof of direct PM management of the arsenal.","contrary_evidence":["The article also notes that some official information can be derived from parliament, budgets, and government statements, so the opacity is relative rather than total."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)","publisher":"CTBTO","publication_date":"n.d.","url":"https://www.ctbto.org/resources/information-materials/frequently-asked-questions","claim":"The CTBTO FAQ states that India is among the Annex 2 states that have not yet signed the CTBT.","locator":"FAQ section on CTBT entry into force and Annex 2 states","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official treaty-body explanation of treaty status; the status is stable and not controversial.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable treaty-status fact relevant to 2022 and unchanged in principle since then.","ruler_attribution":"This is state-level treaty posture under Modi’s government rather than a personal act.","contrary_evidence":["The FAQ also explains that non-signature does not by itself mean a state plans to test, so the treaty status is not direct evidence of reckless intent."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"First Committee Sends 20 Nuclear Weapons Drafts to General Assembly Requiring 33 Separate Votes for Adoption","publisher":"United Nations Press","publication_date":"2022-10-28","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/gadis3701.doc.htm","claim":"The First Committee record shows India abstained on the CTBT resolution as a whole and was the lone no on a paragraph calling for early entry into force of the treaty.","locator":"CTBT voting paragraphs in the press release","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous UN voting record with explicit vote counts and named state positions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly relevant to arms-control support during Modi’s tenure.","ruler_attribution":"India’s vote is a government position, not a private statement by Modi, but it reflects the administration's treaty posture.","contrary_evidence":["India may object to CTBT entry-in-force mechanics on doctrinal grounds, so abstention is not a clean proxy for wanting nuclear testing."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Must Step Back from 'Brink of Nuclear Madness' Amid Growing Risk of Miscalculation, Accidental Use, First Committee Told","publisher":"United Nations Press","publication_date":"2022-10-17","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/gadis3691.doc.htm","claim":"India told the First Committee that it maintained a credible minimum deterrent with no first use and no use against non-nuclear-weapon states, and its 'reducing nuclear danger' resolution emphasized de-alerting and de-targeting.","locator":"India statement summary in the press release","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN summary of India’s position during the 2022 disarmament debate.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly on nuclear-rhetoric and risk-reduction posture.","ruler_attribution":"Government declaratory policy; it indicates the posture Modi’s administration represented internationally.","contrary_evidence":["This is declaratory rhetoric, not proof of implementation or of private PM restraint in a crisis."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.4","1B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Disarmament and International Security Affairs Overview","publisher":"Ministry of External Affairs, Government of India","publication_date":"n.d.","url":"https://www.mea.gov.in/05-disarmament-and-international-security-affairs-overview.htm","claim":"The MEA says India's 2021 comprehensive review under UNSCR 1540 assessed its matrix satisfactorily, and that India pursues a law-based export-control system while supporting FMCT negotiations and other disarmament measures.","locator":"lines 194-206 and 217-220","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official foreign-ministry description of India’s standing nonproliferation and export-control institutions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable institutional context, with explicit reference to the 2021 review that governed the 2022 period.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional government posture under Modi's administration; not uniquely attributable to Modi personally.","contrary_evidence":["This page is self-description and can overstate effectiveness; it does not independently verify enforcement quality."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.6","1B.7","1B.9","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Weapons of Mass Destruction and their Delivery Systems (Prohibition of Unlawful Activities) Amendment Bill, 2022","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-12","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1849999","claim":"The 2022 amendment bill sought to prohibit financing of WMD-related activity and empower the government to freeze or seize assets tied to such financing.","locator":"lines 19 and 94","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary parliamentary/government release describing the enacted policy change in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and relevant to dual-use and proliferation controls.","ruler_attribution":"This is a government policy decision under Modi’s administration, though not a personal technical action by him.","contrary_evidence":["The measure strengthens legal tools but does not prove enforcement success or show that India faced a specific imminent proliferation problem in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.6","1B.9"]}

**Compact synthesis**
The 2022 record points to a mixed posture. Modi had real authority over nuclear release and foreign-policy signaling, but the operational and technical side was institutional and partly opaque. The strongest positive evidence is declaratory restraint, de-alerting/de-targeting language, anti-proliferation controls, and explicit de-escalatory rhetoric on Ukraine. The strongest negative evidence is the March 2022 accidental missile launch into Pakistan and the broader picture of continuing modernization with limited transparency. The year looks more like continuity under a restrained but still risk-bearing nuclear posture than a major risk-reduction breakthrough.

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across PMO/PIB/MEA, SIPRI, Bulletin/FAS, CTBTO/UN, Carnegie, Reuters-syndicated reporting, and Indian parliamentary or regulatory pages. I retained 11 source families above and rejected the rest for one of four reasons.

- Duplicate or overlapping: multiple SIPRI/FAS pages restated the same 2022 inventory and modernization picture; I kept the most direct 2022 inventory assessment plus the stronger Bulletin notebook.
- Out of period: 2023-2026 FAS India notebook updates, 2024 UN AI/disarmament coverage, and other post-2022 pages were not retained unless they described stable treaty status or long-running institutional arrangements.
- Weak or non-substantive: DAE session index pages and parliamentary question lists that only named topics without accessible answer text were not accepted as evidence units.
- Irrelevant to 1B: Agnipath, economic policy, and general defense-industrial pages were reviewed but excluded because they do not materially bear on nuclear or existential-risk responsibility.
- Blocked/timeout: a few UN pages timed out on open; where the search snippet contained the relevant voting or statement summary, I used the snippet rather than forcing a weaker proxy.
- Older contextual material: 1999-2016 doctrine essays and older archival pages were useful background but not retained when a 2022 or stable institutional source was available.

**Remaining Gaps**
- There is no strong public 2022 source showing Modi personally intervened in nuclear command-and-control reform, custody discipline, or appointment choices.
- I did not find a solid 2022 local-language source that directly addressed nuclear-risk responsibility rather than broader defense or energy policy.
- The public record still relies heavily on state self-description and open-source nuclear estimates, so some of the inherited-posture assessment remains approximate rather than fully verified.
